        "knowledge_point": "Finding the Probability of an Intersection",
        "problem": "Given that $P(B)=\\frac{11}{20},$ $P(A\\cap B)=\\frac{1}{4},$ and $P(A\\cup B)=\\frac{3}{4},$ find $P({A}^{′}\\cap B).$",
        "worked_solution": "First, we calculate $P(A)$ using the addition law:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup B) & =P(A)+P(B)-P(A\\cap B) \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & =P(A)+\\frac{11}{20}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ P(A) & =\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{11}{20}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ P(A) & =\\frac{15}{20}-\\frac{11}{20}+\\frac{5}{20} \\\\ P(A) & =\\frac{9}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we construct the corresponding Venn diagram and shade the region ${A}^{′}\\cap B.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041480/assets/b39a37400a7831f642cd6fe1d71db3c518fd348531a09ebc9d601586ed44afa5.png)\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nP({A}^{′}\\cap B)=\\frac{3}{10}.\n$$",
