        "knowledge_point": "Solving Trigonometric Equations Outside of the Range of Cosecant",
        "problem": "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\le x<2\\pi ?$\n\nI. $5-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=4\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$\n\nII. $\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=2-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$\n\nIII. $5\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-3=-2$",
        "worked_solution": "We first simplify the given equations, and get\n\nI. $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=1$\n\nII. $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$\n\nIII. $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{5}$\n\nNow, recall that the range of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equations II and III has no solutions.",
